## slope-efficiency
slope-efficiency-ask = How does a slope’s efficiency change if it is raised higher?
slope-efficiency-title = The efficiency of an inclined plane
slope-efficiency-choices = It grows
slope-efficiency-choices-2 = It falls
slope-efficiency-choices-3 = It stays the same
slope-efficiency-weights-label = Weights on the block (100 g each)
slope-efficiency-with-uk = with one weight
slope-efficiency-with-uk-2 = with two weights
slope-efficiency-with-uk-3 = with three weights
slope-efficiency-build-question = We weigh a wooden block { $with_ } of 100 g on the dynamometer. Then we put it on a 60 cm wooden board, raise its end 10, 20 and 30 cm and each time pull the block steadily up along the board. The efficiency of the slope η = P·h / (F·l). How does it change if the board is raised higher?
slope-efficiency-plan-label = Block { $with_ }
slope-efficiency-answer-why = Lifting the block by h takes the work P·h — straight up or along a slope. Pulling along a board of length l longer than h, the same work is done with a smaller force: without friction F = P·h / l — as many times less than the weight as the board is longer than the height. But the block presses on the board and rubs on it: friction μ·P·cos α adds to the force, and that part of the work goes into heat, not into lifting. The steeper the board, the less the block presses on it (a smaller cos α) — less friction — and the bigger the share of the force that lifts (a larger sin α). So the efficiency grows with the height, and it never reaches 100 %: there is always friction.
slope-efficiency-answer-say = The steeper the slope, the less is lost to friction!
slope-efficiency-answer-text = The block with its weights weighs P = { $P }. { $list }. The higher the board, the higher the efficiency: the block presses on it less (friction is proportional to P·cos α), and a smaller share of the work goes into friction. A slope gains in force — pulling is easier than lifting (F < P) — but loses in distance: the “golden rule of mechanics”. The efficiency is always below 100 %, as there is always friction.
slope-efficiency-setup = A wooden board on a stand, a wooden block, 100 g weights, a dynamometer, a ruler
slope-efficiency-busy = Pulling the block up steadily…
slope-efficiency-say = The block goes up steadily — the dynamometer shows { $F }.
slope-efficiency-board-title = the useful work — lifting the block by h = { $h }
slope-efficiency-board-title-2 = the work done — pulling along the board l = { $l }
slope-efficiency-board-title-3 = the efficiency of the slope (h = { $h })
